                "While cleaning her grandmother's old house, Amira found a <span class='mark'>bundle</span> of old letters in a wooden drawer. The paper was old and fragile, but the handwriting was still elegant and clear. She sat on the floor and began to read, forgetting the dust around her.",
                "The letters were written by a young <span class='mark'>teacher</span> who had left the town many years before. She wrote about a distant city, about her students, and about a promise to return. Every letter ended with the same words: \"I will come back in the spring.\" The pages were thin as leaves, and some words had almost disappeared.",
                "But the teacher never returned. The last letter, dated many years ago, explained that the roads had closed and the journey had become <span class='mark'>impossible</span>. Amira read it twice, and her eyes filled with tears.",
                "Curious, Amira asked about the story, and her mother answered with surprising details: the teacher was part of the family, and her name was still spoken with love. In the attic there were more papers, and <span class='mark'>perhaps</span> even a reply that nobody had ever sent.",
                "For weeks, Amira collected the pieces of the story. She visited a distant city on a rainy day and found the old <span class='mark'>school</span> where the teacher had worked. In the school archive, a list of names confirmed everything she had read.",
