                "In the spring, he traveled to a mountain pass in person. The <span class='mark'>journey</span> was long, but the road itself seemed to answer questions he had carried for years. At sunset, he stood where the old map placed a market square, now a quiet field of grass. Every village he passed had its own stories and its own version of the old route.",
                "Then he saw it: a carved stone, half-hidden under wild plants. It matched a mark on the map that he had once thought was a mistake. He sat beside it, drew every stone in his <span class='mark'>notebook</span>, and corrected the names one by one.",
                "Back home, he finished the map his grandfather had left incomplete. He did not want to invent anything; he only wanted to put the truth in its place. He added the corrected lines in <span class='mark'>careful</span> ink and wrote a short note in the corner: \"Checked by the grandson, in the spring of this year.\"",
                "The map was later shown in the town <span class='mark'>library</span>, next to photographs of the journey. Visitors stopped and pointed at streets they knew. Sami understood that a map is a promise: it says \"this place matters,\" and it asks someone, someday, to go and see."
